1
00:00:00,340 --> 00:00:04,851
You have planned several small fixes, and
the same guidance shows up in your

2
00:00:04,851 --> 00:00:05,385
messages.

3
00:00:05,565 --> 00:00:08,810
Take one example and mark two kinds of
information.

4
00:00:08,990 --> 00:00:12,925
The issue number and target behavior
belong to the current task.

5
00:00:13,105 --> 00:00:17,639
Where tests live and how to run them are
conventions for the repository.

6
00:00:17,819 --> 00:00:22,534
Keeping them in the right place lets a
teammate reuse the guidance without

7
00:00:22,534 --> 00:00:25,082
copying an old issue or its assumptions.

8
00:00:26,548 --> 00:00:31,147
Save this reusable prompt as Markdown in
the training repository.

9
00:00:31,327 --> 00:00:35,972
Each time you use it, fill in the issue
and attach the current source and test

10
00:00:35,972 --> 00:00:36,330
files.

11
00:00:36,510 --> 00:00:38,326
Call out the output you want.

12
00:00:38,506 --> 00:00:43,772
Here the agent should describe the source
path, propose the smallest edit, name the

13
00:00:43,772 --> 00:00:45,930
verification, and stop for review.

14
00:00:46,110 --> 00:00:50,973
A teammate can copy the template into chat
on the approved VS Code setup.

15
00:00:52,423 --> 00:00:54,816
Start Plan and paste the filled template.

16
00:00:54,996 --> 00:00:59,714
Attach the issue, the source file, and the
tests from the revision you intend to

17
00:00:59,714 --> 00:01:00,127
change.

18
00:01:00,307 --> 00:01:02,732
Check the response against those sources.

19
00:01:02,912 --> 00:01:07,878
If the plan talks about a file that does
not exist, ask which available evidence

20
00:01:07,878 --> 00:01:09,119
supports that claim.

21
00:01:09,299 --> 00:01:12,224
These attachments are context for this
request.

22
00:01:12,404 --> 00:01:17,855
A reusable prompt is easier to trust when
the task-specific evidence stays visible.

23
00:01:19,298 --> 00:01:23,840
Open the repository root and confirm the
selected Copilot setup.

24
00:01:24,020 --> 00:01:28,691
In this example, use the project
instructions file in the dot GitHub

25
00:01:28,691 --> 00:01:29,172
folder.

26
00:01:29,352 --> 00:01:33,725
Add the few conventions we want available
when working on this project.

27
00:01:33,905 --> 00:01:38,919
Tell the agent where tests go, which test
framework is already in use, and where to

28
00:01:38,919 --> 00:01:40,731
find the verification command.

29
00:01:40,911 --> 00:01:45,888
If the repository already has project
instructions, review them before adding

30
00:01:45,888 --> 00:01:47,245
overlapping guidance.

31
00:01:48,715 --> 00:01:51,160
Start a fresh session in the same
workspace.

32
00:01:51,340 --> 00:01:55,438
Use the customizations view to verify that
the file is listed.

33
00:01:55,618 --> 00:02:00,386
Where references are available, check that
the file was included in the request.

34
00:02:00,566 --> 00:02:05,174
Then ask for a small plan without
repeating the conventions in your prompt.

35
00:02:05,354 --> 00:02:09,641
Inspect whether it points to the expected
test location and framework.

36
00:02:09,821 --> 00:02:14,836
Check discovery and behavior separately so
you know what to fix if the result

37
00:02:14,836 --> 00:02:15,357
differs.

38
00:02:16,798 --> 00:02:19,329
The template expresses the current
request.

39
00:02:19,509 --> 00:02:23,914
The instruction file supplies the
project's rules when the selected setup

40
00:02:23,914 --> 00:02:24,458
loads it.

41
00:02:24,638 --> 00:02:28,466
Permissions and required checks enforce
separate boundaries.

42
00:02:28,646 --> 00:02:33,864
If your VS Code release offers runnable
prompt files, confirm support for the

43
00:02:33,864 --> 00:02:36,710
selected harness before using that format.

44
00:02:36,890 --> 00:02:40,805
In this lesson the Markdown template works
as text you can paste.

45
00:02:40,985 --> 00:02:45,528
The next lesson turns a repeated procedure
into an invocable skill.

46
00:02:47,007 --> 00:02:50,165
Reuse the template on a second synthetic
issue.

47
00:02:50,345 --> 00:02:54,174
Replace the issue reference and
attachments with the new evidence.

48
00:02:54,354 --> 00:02:59,247
Check that the project convention still
applies and the plan changes where the new

49
00:02:59,247 --> 00:03:00,381
issue calls for it.

50
00:03:00,561 --> 00:03:03,913
Review the prompt template and instruction
diff with a teammate.

51
00:03:04,093 --> 00:03:08,461
You should be able to point to the task
inputs, the convention that carried over,

52
00:03:08,461 --> 00:03:10,995
and the check that would establish
correctness.
